<!--
  /showcase — screens that WORK. A mockup is a component page in disguise;
  these have state: the team list filters as you type, rows select, the
  detail card mutates the list; the fleet deploys walk through their states
  while latency drifts on a timer. Each screen wears a different theme.
-->
<column class="doc-wide" gap="5">
  <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
  <h1>Showcase</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Two working screens. Type in the filter, click a row, remove someone,
    trigger a deploy — everything mutates real state, and the source beside
    each screen is the whole program. Different themes on purpose: the markup
    says what the screen is, never what it looks like.
  </p>

  <h2>Team — filter, select, mutate <small sx="fg:muted-foreground">· theme: paper</small></h2>
  <row class="split" align="stretch">
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Live — type, click rows, remove someone</p>
      <column data-theme-preview="paper" gap="3" sx="p:5 radius:lg border:border bg:background fg:foreground">
        <input placeholder="Filter people" bind:value={query} />
        <column gap="1">
          {#for p of filtered() key={p.id}}
            <row gap="3" align="center"
                 on:click={() => toggle(p.id)}
                 aria-current={selected()?.id === p.id ? 'true' : null}
                 sx="p:2 radius:md cursor:pointer hover:bg:muted current:bg:muted current:ring:primary">
              <avatar></avatar>
              <strong sx="grow:1">{p.name}</strong>
              <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">{p.team}</p>
              <badge tone={p.status === 'active' ? 'success' : 'muted'}>{p.status}</badge>
            </row>
          {/for}
        </column>
        {#if selected()}
          <card motion:enter={{ opacity: 'fade', translate-y: 'spring:snappy' }}
                motion:exit={{ opacity: 'fade-out' }} sx="p:4">
            <row gap="3" align="center">
              <column gap="1" sx="grow:1">
                <strong>{selected().name}</strong>
                <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">{selected().team} · {selected().status}</p>
              </column>
              <button on:click={flipStatus} variant="outline">
                {selected().status === 'active' ? 'Set away' : 'Set active'}
              </button>
              <button on:click={remove} tone="destructive" variant="outline">Remove</button>
            </row>
          </card>
        {/if}
      </column>
    </column>
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Source — the whole program</p>
      <code-block code={code.team}></code-block>
    </column>
  </row>
  <p>
    <code>filteredList</code> and <code>selection</code> are compositions —
    plain functions over signals, shipped with the runtime. The filter, the
    row highlight (<code>current:</code> on <code>aria-current</code>), the
    detail card animating in and out, the list mutations rippling through the
    derived view: none of it is wiring you wrote.
  </p>

  <h2>Fleet — deploys with states <small sx="fg:muted-foreground">· theme: console</small></h2>
  <row class="split" align="stretch">
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Live — latency drifts; press Deploy</p>
      <column data-theme-preview="console" gap="3" sx="p:5 radius:lg border:border bg:background fg:foreground">
        {#for s of services() key={s.id}}
          <card sx="p:4">
            <column gap="2">
              <row gap="2" align="center">
                <badge tone={s.status === 'live' ? 'success' : 'warning'}>{s.status}</badge>
                <strong sx="grow:1">{s.name}</strong>
                <button on:click={() => deploy(s.id)} disabled={s.status === 'rolling'}>
                  {s.status === 'rolling' ? 'Rolling…' : 'Deploy'}
                </button>
              </row>
              <progress value={s.load} max="100"></progress>
              <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground">p99 {s.p99} ms · load {s.load}%</p>
            </column>
          </card>
        {/for}
        <separator></separator>
        <column gap="1">
          {#for d of deploys() key={d}}
            <p sx="text:sm fg:muted-foreground" motion:enter={{ opacity: 'fade', translate-y: 'spring:snappy' }}>{d}</p>
          {/for}
        </column>
      </column>
    </column>
    <column>
      <p class="pane-label">Source — the whole program</p>
      <code-block code={code.fleet}></code-block>
    </column>
  </row>
  <p>
    The deploy walks <code>live → rolling → live</code> through two signal
    writes; the button disables itself off the same state, and the finished
    deploy prepends to the log. The drifting latency is one effect with an
    interval — its cleanup runs when you navigate away, which is why the
    idiom is an effect and not a bare <code>setInterval</code>.
  </p>

  <row class="page-nav" gap="2">
    <a class="btn" href="/why">← Why SUI</a>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/components">Component library →</a>
  </row>
</column>
